WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Our next guest is the
Academy Award-winning filmmake

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:04.304 align:start size:94% position:6%
behind films like
"If Beale Street Could Talk"

00:00:04.304 --> 00:00:04.838 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and "Moonlight."

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:06.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
His new series,
"The Underground Railroad,

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:09.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
is available on
Amazon Prime Video May 14th.

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:11.111 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:20.020 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.689 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Train chugging ]

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:29.162 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:34.968 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:38.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
Please welcome to the show
Barry Jenkins.

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:41.107
I'm so happy you're here, Barry.
How are you?

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:43.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm good, man. I'm good.
It's good to be here.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:46.646 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So this is a fantastic series
that is adapted

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:50.383 size:84% position:42%
from Colson Whitehead's
equally fantastic book

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:54.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
that imagines
the Underground Railroad

00:00:54.154 --> 00:00:55.121 size:81% position:41% line:92%
as an actual railroad.

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:56.656 line:92%
I was embarrassed that as a kid,

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:58.958 align:start size:94% position:6%
when I first heard
the Underground Railroad,

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:01.127 line:92%
I thought that was the case.

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:02.328 align:start size:94% position:6%
Colson admitted
when he was here,

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:04.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
he thought that was
the case, too.

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:06.332
And is it true that that is also
what you thought?

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:10.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That is definitely true,
but I wasn't embarrassed by it

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:11.171 line:92%
I thought it was awesome.

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:13.306 size:78% position:39%
You know, my granddad
was a longshoreman.

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:16.109
I would see him put his hard hat
and his tool belt on

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:16.910 size:78% position:39% line:92%
and steel-toed boots.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so when I heard
the words Underground Railroad

00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:20.613 line:92%
I imagined him underground

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:22.415
or men like him building
these trains and tunnels.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:26.019 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I got to say, Seth,
it made me feel real damn good

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:27.854 size:81% position:41% line:92%
It made me very proud.

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:28.922 line:92%
-And it's such a wonderful story

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:33.093 align:start size:94% position:6%
because obviously it has
a historical accuracy

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:36.262 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the violence
and terror of slavery,

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:39.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
but there's also
this fantasy element to it

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:41.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was that one of the things
that drew you to it,

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.503 line:92%
and is that central theme ke

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:46.106 align:start size:94% position:6%
to what you wanted
to get off the page?

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:48.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, it drew me to it.
And it's key.

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:50.009 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, you know,
I think I might have said

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.545
I imagined men
like my grandfather underground.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:54.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
I didn't imagine it.
I saw it.

00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:56.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so using my tools
as a visual storyteller,

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.552 align:start size:94% position:6%
I told our production designer
Mark Friedberg,

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:02.422 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I didn't want CGI trains,
I didn't want CGI tunnels.

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:05.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
I wanted everything
to be very grounded and real

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:07.427 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, Colson has this joke
that kids

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:08.695
are going to get assigned
the Underground Railroad.

00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:10.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're going to watch the sho
and tell their teachers,

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.800
"The Underground Railroad with
trains running underground."

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.235 align:start size:94% position:6%
In a way,
I do hope that happens,

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:17.770 align:start size:94% position:6%
because when I was a kid
and I imagined these things,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.740 align:start size:94% position:6%
it was very mystical and
magical, but it was also real.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we strove
for the same thing in the show

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:24.811 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So you obviously have
the ability

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:27.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
to talk to Colson throughout
this project.

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:29.182 size:75% position:38% line:92%
With "Beale Street,"

00:02:29.182 --> 00:02:31.351 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, James Baldwin's
no longer with us.

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:34.020 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm wondering which,
as a director,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.756 align:start size:94% position:6%
is more challenging from you
to do the work

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:38.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
without being able
to talk to the author

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:40.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
or to do the work
with the author

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
being able to throw in
his two cents.

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:45.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, it was interesting,
actually.

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:47.534 align:start size:94% position:6%
I thought at the time
that it was

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:49.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
less challenging
doing "Beale Street"

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:51.604 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I couldn't talk
to Mr. Baldwin,

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:53.373 line:92%
so I just stuck to the text.

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think that experience
gave me the courage

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:59.212
to veer from the text wherever I
felt like the show demanded it

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:01.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
And yet I always
checked in with Colson.

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:02.949 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it was kind of
a mixture of the two.

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:04.083 line:92%
It was really nice to ask him,

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:06.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hey, I think I want to go lef
where you went right."

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:09.489 align:start size:94% position:6%
He would always say, "Cool
It's yours. Go ahead."

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:11.224 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
With one exception.

00:03:11.224 --> 00:03:13.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it was really nice to know
that I could trust myself,

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.695 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I could also reach out
to him when necessary.

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:17.096 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So you did say
"With one exception."

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:20.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
You basically have admitte
you had an idea that was so ba

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:21.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Colson said
you couldn't do it,

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:25.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you're not
willing to admit to it yet

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm not willing to admit
to it yet.

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:28.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maybe after the show is ou
for a couple weeks.

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:30.810 size:84% position:42%
But basically, I wanted
to make two characters

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:31.744 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
brother and sister.

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:33.680
But it's one of those things
where it's like you don't know

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
at point A, and by point Z
the brother and sister.

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:37.951 size:81% position:41% line:92%
And Colson was like --

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:40.320 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was the quickest reply
I ever got from him.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.523 line:92%
He was just like, "Nope."

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:44.324 line:92%
-And he's a very nice guy.

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:47.927
So for him to reply that quickly
in the negative, it must have --

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:50.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hey, can I ask you
a question, man?

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-I was watching the band play.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
At first I thought
it was Tom Hanks

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:57.670 size:78% position:39%
playing Fred Armisen
playing Fred Armisen.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is it Fred Armisen,
or is it Tom Hanks

00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:00.607 line:92%
playing Fred "Middle" Armisen?

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:03.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Most days it's Fred,
but Tom begs us.

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:08.081
You know, I think work's
really slowed down for Tom

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.048 line:92%
and so he begs us to come.

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:11.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think the "Middle"
stands for Hanks.

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:14.020 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We're trying to get at leas
one little bit of Hanks

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
in every night
as a favor to him,

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:19.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
because he's a friend
of the show.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:22.262
I want to talk -- you know
because obviously, you know,

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:25.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
this show is very unblinking
as anyone should be

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:27.900 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you're dealing
with the violence of slavery

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:33.373
But it also -- you know,
it's also about the victor

00:04:33.373 --> 00:04:35.775 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
over black trauma.

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:36.843 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Is it important for you

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:38.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you're dealing
with something like this

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:43.716 size:81% position:41%
to also find a way to,
I don't know, put --

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:46.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
have overcoming in it, as well
-It is.

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:49.355 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's why I wanted to do it
as a series

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:50.523 size:84% position:42% line:92%
and not a feature film.

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:51.424 line:92%
I know the very hard images,

00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:54.360
as I call them, can be very loud
and very lasting.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
You could have three
of those and have 40 of these,

00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:58.364 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it still might
not balance out.

00:04:58.364 --> 00:05:00.166 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so it was important,
if I was going to

00:05:00.166 --> 00:05:01.200 line:92%
put the audience through this,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.370
to have some sort of light
some sort of redemption,

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:05.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, for the experience
of watching it,

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:07.373 align:start size:94% position:6%
the same way the character
have redemption

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:08.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
from the experience
of going through it.

00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:11.344
But I think your tee-up of
Liz Cheney is exactly it.

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:13.813
We went through eight year
of people saying one thing

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:17.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then four years of hearing
"Make America great again,

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.351 line:92%
make America great again."

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:19.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's incumbent
of all of us.

00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:21.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's why I love that you
pull these clips,

00:05:21.254 --> 00:05:23.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You said this,
and now you're saying that,"

00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:26.192 align:start size:94% position:6%
to show what America
once was and always has been

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:27.994 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so some of these moments
had to be unflinching

00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:31.497
because obviously if we can sa
make this place great again,

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:34.033 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're not acknowledging
what it once was.

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:37.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You got to do something
that was very, I would imagine

00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:39.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
cathartic,
which is you got to blow up,

00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:43.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
using dynamite,
a Confederate monument.

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:47.413
-I did, although maybe
I should stop saying that

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:49.816
because I don't know what rights
or restrictions

00:05:49.816 --> 00:05:51.150 size:78% position:39% line:92%
they can place on us.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.085 line:92%
And it's a figurative blowup

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:53.953 align:start size:94% position:6%
We actually didn't
get to blow up pieces of rock.

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:56.622 align:start size:94% position:6%
We brought rock
that we exploded on the face

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:58.157 line:92%
of Stone Mountain, Georgia

00:05:58.157 --> 00:06:01.394 size:81% position:41%
But, yes, the same way
walking on plantations

00:06:01.394 --> 00:06:03.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
and feeling the essence
of my ancestors,

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:05.698 size:75% position:38%
standing on the side
of that structure,

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:07.300 align:start size:94% position:6%
knowing what was etched
on one side

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:08.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
and knowing the story
we were telling,

00:06:08.768 --> 00:06:10.136 line:92%
I did get great satisfaction

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:12.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
over saying, "No,
two takes isn't enough.

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:13.206 size:78% position:39% line:92%
I think we need four.

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:14.374 align:start size:94% position:6%
Let's do it again.
Let's go it again."

00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:17.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, but let's make sure
legally people know

00:06:17.276 --> 00:06:19.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you did not
destroy anything.

00:06:19.445 --> 00:06:22.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Exactly.
-You used the magic

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:24.217 align:start size:94% position:6%
of filmmaking to do that.
-Exactly.

00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:29.822
-You know, you obviously are now
a very accomplished filmmaker,

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:31.457 line:92%
but this was not something

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:34.694 size:84% position:42%
that you had your eyes
set on earlier in life.

00:06:34.694 --> 00:06:37.797 align:start size:94% position:6%
You kind of backed
into being a film student

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:39.065 size:88% position:44% line:92%
when you got to college.

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:41.200
-Yeah, yeah, that's true.
I was at Florida State

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.569 size:75% position:38%
and had some friends
who played football,

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:45.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the football stadium
has a cafeteria

00:06:45.972 --> 00:06:49.041
that's much better than
the cafeteria for everyone else.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:50.143 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they don't tell you,
but they --

00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:52.578
They can't legally say, "Onl
football players eat there."

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:55.948
So my buddies and I, we used t
go eat with the football players

00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:58.184 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the film school
is also in the stadium.

00:06:58.184 --> 00:07:02.054
So I kept walking by the sig
just trying to get really good

00:07:02.054 --> 00:07:04.824 align:start size:94% position:6%
like, mashed potatoes
and macaroni and cheese.

00:07:04.824 --> 00:07:06.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then through
those carbohydrates,

00:07:06.659 --> 00:07:07.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
I saw this sign
that said film school

00:07:07.894 --> 00:07:10.463
and thought, "You know what?
I think I want to try that."

00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:14.767
And, Seth, it's crazy how making
such a random choice

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:17.503 align:start size:94% position:6%
has made probably the bigges
difference in my life.

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:21.073 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I was a film major, so I ha
the opposite path of you,

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:22.642 size:75% position:38%
which I knew exactly
what I wanted to do.

00:07:22.642 --> 00:07:25.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then like two classes in
I realized,

00:07:25.211 --> 00:07:26.546 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh, this is
way too hard for me.

00:07:26.546 --> 00:07:29.649 align:start size:94% position:6%
I do not have the attentio
to detail for this,"

00:07:29.649 --> 00:07:32.118
because I think a lot of people,
young people think,

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:34.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh, my God, making movies
would be so much fun."

00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:37.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then you show up
and you realize, you know,

00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:40.793 align:start size:94% position:6%
it is this really
detail-oriented craft.

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:42.895 line:92%
Did you take to it right away?

00:07:42.895 --> 00:07:43.996 line:92%
-I didn't take to it right away.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:46.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was a guy in my class,
a good friend of mine,

00:07:46.833 --> 00:07:47.533 size:88% position:44% line:92%
this guy named Wes Ball,

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:48.734 size:78% position:39%
who directed all the
"Maze Runner" movies.

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:52.738
He's doing the new "Planet o
the Apes" film, really cool cat.

00:07:52.738 --> 00:07:54.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was, like,
just gifted from jump,

00:07:54.874 --> 00:07:56.943 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I would have to show
my stuff beside his.

00:07:56.943 --> 00:07:59.412
And it was like, "Oh, I can't do
what that guy does."

00:07:59.412 --> 00:08:01.080 size:81% position:41%
But then I realized --
The professor told me

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.748 align:start size:94% position:6%
we all start
with an empty frame.

00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:05.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if you really take the tim
to learn the craft,

00:08:05.151 --> 00:08:07.153 align:start size:94% position:6%
you can carry your voice
with that frame

00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:08.154 line:92%
the same way as anyone else.

00:08:08.154 --> 00:08:09.755 align:start size:94% position:6%
And again,
it was one of those things

00:08:09.755 --> 00:08:11.991 align:start size:94% position:6%
where I convinced myself
I couldn't do it,

00:08:11.991 --> 00:08:16.095
like you said, and then told
myself, "That's not acceptable."

00:08:16.095 --> 00:08:17.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so I took a year off
from film school

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:19.499 align:start size:94% position:6%
and taught myself how to
make still portraits

00:08:19.499 --> 00:08:21.534 size:78% position:39%
and things like that,
and then I came back

00:08:21.534 --> 00:08:22.935 line:92%
and I was able to put my voice

00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:24.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
into the voice
of these other characters.

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:28.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I was like,
"Yo, no one can ever tell me

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:30.243 line:92%
I can't do something again."

00:08:30.243 --> 00:08:31.777 line:92%
And the detail required in thi

00:08:31.777 --> 00:08:34.680 align:start size:94% position:6%
is very liberating,
very freeing.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Did you remember
or do you remember

00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:39.285 line:92%
the first thing you put on film?

00:08:39.285 --> 00:08:41.587 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I do. The first thing
I put a film was --

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:44.056 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's crazy because this
announcement came out abou

00:08:44.056 --> 00:08:45.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
Barry Jenkins,
one of the four directors

00:08:45.892 --> 00:08:47.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
who might direct
the black Superman.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:49.195 align:start size:94% position:6%
The first thing
I ever put on film

00:08:49.195 --> 00:08:52.465
on a Bolex camera was my
roommate, this guy named Jarrod.

00:08:52.465 --> 00:08:56.536 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we had this two-liter
plastic keg thing,

00:08:56.536 --> 00:08:58.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I put a cape on him
and had him run

00:08:58.404 --> 00:08:59.805 align:start size:94% position:6%
in our parking lot
and attack it.

00:08:59.805 --> 00:09:02.542
And that was the very firs
image I ever put on film.

00:09:02.542 --> 00:09:04.477
Very interesting for a guy
who goes on to make movies

00:09:04.477 --> 00:09:07.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
like "Moonlight" and this show
but there it is.

00:09:07.146 --> 00:09:09.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I will say that I think
every film student,

00:09:09.448 --> 00:09:13.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
their first film stars
their roommate.

00:09:13.085 --> 00:09:15.588
-Exactly.
-I think that's very consistent.

00:09:15.588 --> 00:09:19.358
You recently tweeted about
a show you've been watching,

00:09:19.358 --> 00:09:22.728
which I think maybe would not be
people's first guess

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:25.398
as to what you've been watching,
which is a cartoon

00:09:25.398 --> 00:09:29.769 size:75% position:38%
that aired from 2004
to I think like 2011

00:09:29.769 --> 00:09:30.937 line:92%
called "The Backyardigans.

00:09:30.937 --> 00:09:34.440
How did this come about and wh
are you talking about it now

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.642 size:75% position:38%
-I was on the couch,
and I'll admit now

00:09:36.642 --> 00:09:37.310 line:92%
I was drinking some wine.

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:39.745
I was on the couch and I was
scrolling through Twitter,

00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:42.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
and someone posted one
of these songs from this sho

00:09:42.114 --> 00:09:44.083 size:78% position:39%
"The Backyardigans,"
and I was like, "Yo."

00:09:44.083 --> 00:09:47.753 align:start size:94% position:6%
Little buzzed, and I was like,
"This song is amazing,"

00:09:47.753 --> 00:09:48.788 line:92%
and so the dog is with me.

00:09:48.788 --> 00:09:51.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
I load it up on Apple TV.
I bought a season.

00:09:51.424 --> 00:09:52.258 size:75% position:38% line:92%
I began watching it.

00:09:52.258 --> 00:09:53.926 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
these are such heavy times

00:09:53.926 --> 00:09:56.228
and I'm glad you can bring
some humor to that heaviness

00:09:56.228 --> 00:09:58.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
especially with
your intro tonight.

00:09:58.130 --> 00:09:58.965 line:92%
But they're such heavy times

00:09:58.965 --> 00:10:01.233
I was like, "This is such
a beautiful, light thing.

00:10:01.233 --> 00:10:03.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm actually enjoying it.
Why can't I enjoy it?

00:10:03.402 --> 00:10:05.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know I'm 41.
This is meant for 5-year-olds.

00:10:05.471 --> 00:10:07.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
But still," and then the nex
day was Mother's Day,

00:10:07.873 --> 00:10:11.677
and I just tweeted out 'caus
I got on a rant, a drunken ran

00:10:11.677 --> 00:10:12.545 line:92%
about how great the show was

00:10:12.545 --> 00:10:14.680
I tweeted out a photo of the
woman who created the show

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:16.916 align:start size:94% position:6%
who was a black woman,
I didn't realize,

00:10:16.916 --> 00:10:17.950 line:92%
and, Seth, it was amazing.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:19.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
There were people
all over the world

00:10:19.885 --> 00:10:23.322
who were thanking this woman for
helping them raise their kids.

00:10:23.322 --> 00:10:26.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I just thought someone els
maybe could have done this

00:10:26.459 --> 00:10:28.761 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, you know,
here's my platform

00:10:28.761 --> 00:10:29.562 line:92%
and I just want to celebrate

00:10:29.562 --> 00:10:31.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
this really beautiful
piece of work.

00:10:31.631 --> 00:10:33.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, yes, I am now
a "Backyardigans" stan.

00:10:33.833 --> 00:10:35.868 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'll admit,
I'm into the third season now.

00:10:35.868 --> 00:10:38.270
It's pretty dope. I recommend it
to all your viewers.

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:41.440 size:84% position:42%
-I will say, if there's
one thing I can agree

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:42.908 line:92%
that Twitter is best for,

00:10:42.908 --> 00:10:45.711 size:75% position:38%
it is people saying,
"I like this thing."

00:10:45.711 --> 00:10:48.114 line:92%
And if that was all Twitter was,

00:10:48.114 --> 00:10:50.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
it would be a much nicer place
to hang out.

00:10:50.449 --> 00:10:52.385 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hey, really appreciate
you spending time

00:10:52.385 --> 00:10:53.085 size:78% position:39% line:92%
with us today, Barry.

00:10:53.085 --> 00:10:55.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
Congrats on the show.
-Thank you, man. My pleasure

00:10:55.254 --> 00:10:57.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
And thank you for that time
met you on the red carpet, man

00:10:57.657 --> 00:10:59.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
You always been
a real solid dude.

00:10:59.058 --> 00:10:59.692 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Appreciate you.

00:10:59.692 --> 00:11:00.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm hoping next time
it's in person.

00:11:00.993 --> 00:11:02.595 align:start size:94% position:6%
"The Underground Railroad"
is available

00:11:02.595 --> 00:11:05.031 line:92%
on Amazon Prime Video May 14th

